{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Product intent\n2\t\n3\t## Purpose\n4\t\n5\tTurnbridge lets a developer continue a visible coding conversation in a\n6\tdifferent agent CLI when they choose to switch providers or tools. It retains\n7\tthe full user-visible turn history—messages, tool calls, and tool results—\n8\trather than requiring a last-minute summary from a rate-limited model.\n9\t\n10\tIt serves two users who are often the same person on different days: a solo\n11\tdeveloper hopping CLIs mid-task (rate limits, model choice), and small\n12\tcollaborating groups whose members use *different* CLIs and want to pick up\n13\teach other's conversations. Sharing rides on Conversation Ledger's git-notes\n14\tstorage; each turn is attributed to its human author via the repo's git\n15\tidentity, and listings show only your own (plus unattributed) conversations\n16\tunless you ask for everyone's.\n17\t\n18\t## Behavioral commitments\n19\t\n20\t- Capture every available visible turn incrementally, not only on clean exit\n21\t  (delivered by Conversation Ledger's hooks; see division below).\n22\t- Preserve source content faithfully and label its originating CLI.\n23\t- Offer one resume picker across supported CLIs while making the session type\n24\t  honest: native resume for same-CLI records, native-format fabrication for\n25\t  cross-CLI records, transcript rehydration as the always-available fallback.\n26\t- Prefer conversations whose repository and code state match the current work;\n27\t  warn rather than silently treating mismatched history as current.\n28\t- Never claim to transfer hidden reasoning, private provider state, or an\n29\t  exact native session; a fabricated session is labeled as imported. The one\n30\t  exception is verifiably real, not claimed: a Codex-origin conversation\n31\t  fabricated back into Codex replays its provider-encrypted `reasoning`\n32\t  blobs verbatim by default (never across CLIs — ciphertext can't be forged\n33\t  as a foreign CLI's native reasoning), and the import notice discloses\n34\t  exactly how many blocks were replayed so the label stays honest either way.\n35\t  `--no-reasoning-replay` / `{\"reasoningReplay\": false}` opts out.\n36\t- Put the developer in control of capture, sharing, and deletion. Sharing is\n37\t  a deliberate choice, not a side effect: today it is an explicit\n38\t  `cledger sync`; the decided direction (see Conversation Ledger's design\n39\t  doc) is optional-but-default-on transport hooks so records ride normal\n40\t  `git push`/`pull`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\n41\t  user. Listings default to the current user's records.\n42\t\n43\t## Non-goals\n44\t\n45\t- A team presence system, task tracker, or agent orchestration framework.\n46\t- Inferring or replacing human intent.\n47\t- Summarized \"handoff notes\" (sticky-note-style); Turnbridge moves the literal\n48\t  record.\n49\t\n50\t## Relationship to Conversation Ledger\n51\t\n52\tTurnbridge is a client of Conversation Ledger, which owns the durable neutral\n53\trecord **and** the source-CLI capture adapters (hooks, transcript parsers,\n54\tcursors, dedup). Turnbridge owns everything on the resume side: the unified\n55\tpicker, ownership/compatibility filtering, target-CLI session fabrication,\n56\tbootstrap rehydration, and the optional command shims.\n57\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QEVhmye86qvepa7d8Av4aN","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"defab1d48ffb334ef077b543c74110f5a05efd71","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","seq":39},"id":"ev1-cdca9268266f1f79f28021fb5acbe1671be1737396803ccf7ec24a1064b8a087","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-12T01:20:41.170Z","producer":{"session_id":"60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab