a the lineage chain when\n263\t  re-bridging) is deliberately not built and stays a follow-up. Never\n264\t  applies to a Claude Code target: foreign reasoning can't be forged as a\n265\t  native reasoning item, so the only real switch is whether provider-matched\n266\t  replay happens, not what it's coerced into — unreadable/foreign reasoning\n267\t  is still folded into labeled text as before. On by default (decided\n268\t  2026-07-23); opt out with `--no-reasoning-replay` or\n269\t  `{\"reasoningReplay\": false}` in `~/.turnbridge/config.json` (`src/config.ts`).\n270\t  No ownership gate — replay applies to any synced conversation, not just\n271\t  the current user's own, per 2026-07-23 decision. The fabricated session's\n272\t  import notice (`buildRolloutLines`) is conditional: it discloses the\n273\t  replayed-block count when replay happened instead of the blanket \"hidden\n274\t  reasoning ... not transferred\" claim, and still states that claim for\n275\t  everything else in the same session. Empirically verified via\n276\t  scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs (manual injection, independent of\n277\t  this integration): same-account and cross-account replay of a real blob in\n278\t  a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI versions, and a\n279\t  different paid ChatGPT account (identical token accounting), so the\n280\t  ciphertext is not keyed per-account; the free tier cannot run Codex at all.\n281\t  Still open: blob TTL, and whether API-platform-org auth behaves the same\n282\t  as ChatGPT auth — turnbridge attempts replay unconditionally either way\n283\t  since auth mode isn't observable from here; a rejected blob at actual\n284\t  `codex resume` time has no proactive detection and falls under the same\n285\t  `--bootstrap` escape hatch as any other fabrication-drift failure.\n286\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_016ibQ2LDDEZhhok3snU8r1s","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"defab1d48ffb334ef077b543c74110f5a05efd71","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","seq":40},"id":"ev1-32b482d13a3de29c074d4f0cfb29dbd2f8f2941833f071821aa20f7c872badd8","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-12T01:20:41.171Z","producer":{"session_id":"60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.228","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# WIP technical design\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** MVP implemented for Claude Code ⇄ Codex ⇄ opencode; fabrication\n4\tadapters are version-pinned and must be revalidated per CLI release.\n5\t\n6\t## Main flow\n7\t\n8\t1. Conversation Ledger's hooks (`cledger install all`) capture each CLI's\n9\t   local transcript incrementally into git notes as normalized\n10\t   `conversation_turn` events, stamped with the repo's git identity\n11\t   (`actor.id` = `user.email`) on human turns. opencode has no transcript file\n12\t   to tail — cledger captures it through a `session.idle` plugin that converts\n13\t   `opencode export`, one event per *part*, since its store is mutable\n14\t   (cledger 0.18.0+).\n15\t2. `turnbridge resume [claude|codex|opencode]` lists conversations compatible with the\n16\t   current repository state: events anchored to commits reachable from `HEAD`\n17\t   (`--any-commit` lifts this), authored by you or unattributed\n18\t   (`--all` includes collaborators).\n19\t3. Selecting a conversation whose source matches the target CLI delegates to\n20\t   that CLI's native resume.\n21\t4. Cross-CLI selections default to **native fabrication**: turnbridge writes a\n22\t   target-native session file from the canonical events and launches the\n23\t   target's own resume on it. `--bootstrap` (also the automatic fallback when\n24\t   fabrication is unsupported or fails validation) starts a fresh target\n25\t   session whose first prompt instructs it to read the literal transcript.\n26\t\n27\t## Module map\n28\t\n29\t- `src/conversations.ts` — ledger read + grouping + ownership/compat filter.\n30\t- `src/resume.ts` — picker flow, target choice, default-target config.\n31\t- `src/targets/*` — per-CLI `TargetAdapter`: `nativeResume`, `fabricate`,\n32\t  `bootstrap`. Fabrication throws `FabricationUnsupportedError` to trigger the\n33\t  fallback.\n34\t- `src/transcript.ts` + `src/bootstrap.ts` — literal markdown rendering and\n35\t  the rehydration prompt; reports estimated token size before launch.\n36\t- `src/shim.ts` — opt-in PATH shims: bare `claude --resume` / `codex resume`\n37\t  open the merged picker; everything else passes through to the real binary.\n38\t  No opencode shim: it has no argument-free \"show me my sessions\" invocation to\n39\t  interpose on (`-s` always takes an id), and intercepting one that takes an\n40\t  argument would both discard the id and recurse through the shim.\n41\t\n42\t## Division of labor with Conversation Ledger\n43\t\n44\tThe ledger owns capture (adapters, cursors, idempotent event ids, git-notes\n45\tstorage, transport). Turnbridge never parses source transcripts itself and\n46\tis a reader plus a writer of *target-native* session files. It writes exactly\n47\tone kind of ledger event — `continuation` lineage edges (below) — and no\n48\tconversation content. Ledger changes made for turnbridge: a library entry\n49\tpoint (`package.json` exports) and human-turn identity stamping.\n50\t\n51\tTurnbridge's reads inherit cledger 0.8.0's read-time behavior: `readEvents()`\n52\tlazily absorbs whatever transport already fetched into the local ref, then\n53\tauto-runs squash/rebase re-anchor detection, which can append a `re_anchor`\n54\tmapping event and print a notice to stderr. Reachability filtering resolves\n55\tthrough `re_anchor` mappings, so a bridged conversation and its `continuation`\n56\tlineage survive a squash merge instead of falling out of the picker. Turnbridge\n57\tissues two such reads per invocation (conversation listing, then lineage\n58\tlookup) and buffers the ledger's stderr notices across both so they render as\n59\tone block above the picker rather than interleaving with it.\n60\t\n61\t## Conversation lineage (branch-on-bridge)\n62\t\n63\tBridging is a fork, not a move. Fabricating a target session writes a real\n64\tnative file; when the user continues there, the target CLI captures it as a\n65\tnew conversation whose id is the session id turnbridge generated (verified:\n66\tCodex resume preserves the id and appends). The source conversation still\n67\texists, so both are resumable — a genuine branch, like a git fork.\n68\t\n69\tTo keep that honest instead of confusing, at fabricate time turnbridge appends\n70\ta `continuation` event (`links: [{rel:\"continues\", target:<source>}]`, content\n71\tcarries `imported_through_seq`) belonging to the target conversation. The\n72\tpicker reads these and labels rows in both directions (`→ bridged to Codex`,\n73\t`↳ continued from Claude Code`); re-bridging a conversation that already\n74\tembeds imported history is allowed but flagged. The default listing shows\n75\tboth branch endpoints — abandonment is not assumed. The same edge is what lets\n76\ta downstream consumer (intent-recall) de-duplicate the copied prefix: target\n77\tturns `0..imported_through_seq` are known copies of the source, not fresh\n78\tevidence, so turnbridge does not need to alter capture to avoid double-count.\n79\t\n80\t## Fabrication contract (per target adapter)\n81\t\n82\t**Governing rule: the fabricated session has to work in the target.** Fidelity\n83\tto the source is a property of a session that functions, not a substitute for\n84\tone. Where a target's native records demand a particular shape — a resolvable\n85\tprovider id, a specific part structure — match that shape, and carry the\n86\tsource's identity somewhere that does not break resume. A bridged session that\n87\tis unimpeachably honest and cannot be continued has failed at the only thing it\n88\texists to do. *(Corrected 2026-08-06, after `providerID: \"turnbridge\"` silently\n89\tbricked continuation on every bridged opencode session — see the opencode notes\n90\tbelow.)*\n91\t\n92\tEvery field a fabricated session carries falls into one of three tiers, and the\n93\ttier decides where the logic lives:\n94\t\n95\t1. **Portable** — the same meaning in every target: turn role, visible text,\n96\t   ordering, timestamps. Shared code writes these once; adapters do not touch\n97\t   them.\n98\t2. **Portable by default, overridden where a target requires it** — usually\n99\t   transferable, but some targets reject or mishandle the generic form, so the\n100\t   adapter substitutes a working value and discloses the substitution. The\n101\t   source model id is the type case; foreign tool calls are the other\n102\t   (structured where the target accepts unknown names, labeled text where not).\n103\t3. **Target-specific** — no portable form exists and the adapter owns it\n104\t   outright: opencode's `providerID`/`projectID` and its `step-start`/\n105\t   `step-finish` part scaffolding, Claude's `parentUuid` chain, Codex's paired\n106\t   `event_msg`/`response_item` twins.\n107\t\n108\tTiers 2 and 3 are what the adapter abstraction exists for. Discovering that a\n109\tfield assumed to be tier 1 is really tier 2 is a contract change worth\n110\trecording here, not a quiet workaround inside one adapter.\n111\t\n112\t- Pin the CLI versions the writer was validated against; on an unknown version\n113\t  warn and offer bootstrap.\n114\t- Write only new session files under the CLI's own session directory; never\n115\t  modify existing native sessions.\n116\t- Represent foreign tool calls faithfully (or as clearly-labeled text when the\n117\t  target rejects unknown tool schemas — see per-adapter spec notes).\n118\t- Propagate the source model id into fabricated assistant envelopes verbatim\n119\t  **where the target can resolve it** (decided 2026-07-21, amended 2026-08-06).\n120\t  If the target recognizes it — e.g. the user registered matching external\n121\t  models in Claude Code — the session restores seamlessly. If it does not, write\n122\t  a value the target *can* resolve and disclose the real source model in the\n123\t  import notice; do not leave an unresolvable id in a field the target uses to\n124\t  dispatch. The original rule (\"never substitute a recognized-but-false id\")\n125\t  was written to keep the transcript honest and instead produced sessions that\n126\t  could not be continued at all; honesty belongs in the disclosure, not in a\n127\t  dispatch field.\n128\t\n129\tReverse-engineered, empirically verified format specs (pinned versions,\n130\tminimal working schemas, unknowns): [docs/specs/claude-session-format.md](specs/claude-session-format.md),\n131\t[docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md](specs/codex-rollout-format.md), and\n132\t[docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md](specs/opencode-session-format.md).\n133\tKey constraints: Claude resume is scoped to the project dir encoded from the\n134\tlaunch cwd and reconstructs history by walking `parentUuid` from the last\n135\tline; Codex resume-by-id scans `~/.codex/sessions` for a matching filename +\n136\t`session_meta` id, and only plain `message` response_items are verified-safe\n137\tto replay (foreign tool calls fold into labeled text). Claude accepts foreign\n138\t`tool_use` names verbatim, so Codex→Claude keeps structured tool history.\n139\t\n140\topencode is the first target that breaks the \"write a new session file\"\n141\tassumption: every session lives in one shared SQLite DB, so fabrication builds\n142\ta payload in opencode's own export format and shells out to `opencode import`.\n143\tIt is also the target that taught the tier model its lesson twice over: the\n144\