{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Worktree is on `design/policy-split`, main checkout untouched. Line citations spot-checked against the files.\n\n**Found the note:** it's the \"Fabrication contract\" section, `docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:92-110`, written 2026-08-06 right after the `providerID: \"turnbridge\"` incident. Worth knowing it's a post-mortem generalization from a single incident — that shows in its shape.\n\nAnalysis is in `docs/POLICY_SPLIT.md` in the worktree. Direct answers:\n\n**Three-way, but your stated axis is the wrong one — which is exactly why it reads like it might be two.** As written, tiers 2 and 3 differ by *degree of overridability*, and on that axis it is binary, since both end with the adapter deciding. Your has-defaults/no-defaults guess is the natural next step and it's also not quite true: tier-3 fields mostly *do* have defaults (`providerID` defaults to first resolvable, `parentUuid` has a construction rule). They aren't undefaulted, they're **unsourced**. The axis that genuinely yields three is *where the value comes from*: **carried** (source verbatim) / **negotiated** (source if the target accepts it, else degraded) / **constructed** (source never had one). The payoff is that **tier 2 is the only tier that owes a disclosure**, because it's the only one where a source fact changed. Tier 3 loses nothing — `parentUuid` isn't a degraded version of anything. Calling it \"target-specific\" makes it sound like the most lossy tier when it's as lossless as tier 1.\n\n**The bigger finding: tiers attach to `(field × target)`, not to fields.** The doc says \"the model id is tier 2.\" The code says three different things about that one canonical field — Claude propagates it verbatim, opencode resolves-or-substitutes-and-discloses, Codex never writes it at all. None is wrong in isolation: Claude's `model` is cosmetic (your own spec, `claude-session-format.md:243`), opencode's `providerID` is *dispatch*. So the real discriminator under tier 2 is **does the target read this field back to decide behavior, or merely record it?** That was the actual lesson of the opencode incident; the note generalized it one notch too wide.\n\n**One live gap that falls straight out of this:** Claude→Codex silently discards which model produced every historical turn, and the Codex import notice never mentions it (`codex.ts:192-199`). By the contract's own rule that's an undisclosed tier-2 degradation. Small loss, but it's the exact class the tier model exists to prevent, and it slipped because the rule lives in prose adapters are trusted to follow. Same for the human author's git identity, dropped on all three paths.\n\n**Design: not a unified engine, but not just a principle either.** Unify tier 1 (one portable-turn normalizer — role mapping, ordering, `pickerTitle` are written 3× today) and unify the *accounting* of tier 2 (a degradation ledger the import notice is **derived from**, so an undisclosed substitution stops being expressible). Leave tier 2's *mechanics* and all of tier 3 adapter-private — the three containers share nothing, and an abstraction there is a lie you'll pay for while debugging. Then a cross-adapter conformance suite asserting tier-1 invariants, generalizing `fabricate-invariants.test.ts`. That's how you keep tier 1 honest without unifying writers.\n\n**Where it cuts against the project's aims** — the thing I'd most want you to react to: the tier model is a *concession framework*, and concessions ratchet one way. Once a field is labeled tier 2, \"substitute and disclose\" is always the cheap path, because disclosure is free. The 2026-08-06 amendment (\"honesty belongs in the disclosure, not in a dispatch field\") was right for its case and is a general-purpose excuse everywhere else. If you keep the model, it needs a ratchet: 1→2 only on *demonstrated* target failure recorded with a CLI version, adapters must attempt the portable value first (opencode's `resolveModel` is the pattern), and the tier-2 count is debt to drive down, not stable architecture. Also: the import notice is a user turn at the top of the target's context — disclosures have a real budget, and it's drifting toward completeness-record while being read as minimum-necessary.\n\nTwo open questions I'd put back to you rather than assume: whether actor identity *should* transfer into target sessions at all (product call, currently an unanswered silent drop), and whether \"tier\" covers capture too — cledger flattening opencode reasoning to plaintext is a tier-2 degradation on the read side, outside this contract's scope, which will make round-trip conversations slide between two meanings of \"portable.\"\n\nAlso noted for §6: \"Shared code writes these once; adapters do not touch them\" (`:97`) isn't true of the code today — either make it true or restate it as target state.\n\nresult: Located the three-tier note (`WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md:92-110`) and wrote `docs/POLICY_SPLIT.md` on branch `design/policy-split` — argues the split is genuinely 3-way but mis-axed (should be carried/negotiated/constructed, with disclosure as tier 2's defining obligation), that tiers attach to `(field × target)` not fields, proposes a shared normalizer + derived-disclosure ledger with tier 3 left private, and flags a live undisclosed model-id drop on the Claude→Codex path.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"defab1d48ffb334ef077b543c74110f5a05efd71","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","seq":114},"id":"ev1-de2974c3f0d4ebaa5299ec356d61208cfd166c32312659e08839e1c12d59bc01","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-12T01:24:34.221Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.228","tool":"cledger","version":"0.2